<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In recent years, vision-language models (VLMs) have made substantial progress in visual understanding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
However, this progress remains uneven between high-level semantic understanding and fine-grained visual understanding: while VLMs are comparatively strong in high-level semantic understanding, they remain substantially less reliable on fine-grained tasks involving objects, attributes, counting, and spatial relations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tong et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kamoi et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
Existing methods mainly improve fine-grained visual understanding through higher-fidelity visual encoding and more information-preserving vision–language interfaces <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">You et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tong et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, but still rely on language-aligned vision encoders that compress visual content into limited representations before language modeling, so that fine-grained evidence lost upstream becomes unavailable downstream.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">UMMs jointly model multimodal understanding and generation within a single model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.
Compared with VLMs that rely primarily on language-aligned visual representations, the generation pathway in UMMs typically preserves richer structural information, spatial relations, and local details <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>.
Meanwhile, the understanding and generation pathways share knowledge and mutually adapt during joint modeling, providing a basis for complementary use of the two types of visual information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.
However, fine-grained visual understanding remains challenging for UMMs, and it remains unclear whether and under what conditions generation-side representations provide additional evidence for fine-grained understanding, as well as how such evidence can be effectively exploited by the understanding pathway <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2026a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Generation-assisted understanding in UMMs mainly follows two directions.
One line uses generative supervision to shape visual representations or internal hidden states to encode richer fine-grained visual information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luo et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>.
However, these methods do not directly examine or exploit information in native generation-side representations.
Their heuristic supervision targets limit task-conditioned visual evidence selection, while differing representational requirements of understanding and generation may introduce representational conflicts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Su et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.
Another line explicitly generates visual intermediate states at inference time and uses them for downstream understanding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Su et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
However, this approach externalizes generation-side information into limited intermediate states, with some methods re-encoding the outputs into the understanding space and reintroducing the visual encoding bottleneck of the understanding pathway <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hao et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.
Explicit generation introduces inference overhead and may propagate generation errors into downstream understanding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meng et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To address these limitations, we first examine how generation-side representations contribute to fine-grained understanding.
We focus on whether they contain visual cues beyond those captured by the understanding pathway and on the factors that determine this advantage.
We find that generation-side representations respond more strongly to subtle semantic changes, capture changes missed by understanding-side representations, and rely on spatial organization to retain this advantage.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Guided by these findings, we propose GAFU, a Generation-Assisted Fine-grained Understanding post-training framework that directly exploits native generation-side representations.
To preserve fine-grained information, GAFU transforms native generation-side representations into compact <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> tokens through compression, spatial binding, and relation alignment.
We then use the evolving text states to selectively retrieve task-relevant evidence from <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and feed it back into the understanding pathway.
We further use pairwise preference learning to encourage reliance on sample-relevant generation-side evidence while preserving the original understanding capability.
We evaluate GAFU across UMMs with diverse architectures and scales on general, spatial, and domain-specific visual understanding tasks.
GAFU outperforms same-data SFT in all 15 model–benchmark combinations, with substantial average gains of 3.56–5.98 percentage points across the five benchmarks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our contributions can be summarized as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We systematically study understanding- and generation-side visual representations in UMMs, revealing generation-side advantages in fine-grained semantic sensitivity, representational complementarity, and spatial dependence.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on these findings, we propose GAFU, which enables the understanding pathway to exploit complementary generation-side information through representation compression and alignment, task-conditioned injection, and pairwise preference learning.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We validate GAFU across diverse UMM architectures and visual understanding tasks, outperforming same-data SFT in all 15 settings by 3.56–5.98 percentage points on average.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Unified Multimodal Models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">UMMs jointly model multimodal understanding and visual generation within a single model through autoregressive, diffusion-based, or hybrid modeling paradigms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">You et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.
Early UMMs often relied on a unified dense backbone with shared visual representations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024c</a>)</cite>, while later designs increasingly specialize the visual pathways for understanding and generation within a unified framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.
This evolution reflects different representation needs: understanding relies more on high-level semantics, whereas generation requires richer visual details, motivating many UMMs to use language-aligned encoders such as CLIP or SigLIP for understanding and VAE- or VQ-VAE-based encoders for generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.
Despite this progress, architectural unification has not yet translated into effective synergy between understanding and generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, with persistent capability gaps <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026</a>)</cite> and limited generation-to-understanding transfer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Understanding–Generation Collaboration in Unified Multimodal Models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Collaboration between understanding and generation in UMMs mainly follows two directions: understanding-guided generation and generation-assisted understanding.
Understanding-guided generation uses understanding-side information for generation conditioning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, task planning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeng et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2026b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, and output feedback <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jin et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>, forming a relatively mature paradigm.
In contrast, generation-assisted understanding has received comparatively less systematic attention.
It mainly relies on generative supervision to shape visual representations or internal states <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>, which can constrain representation learning through indirect objectives, or on generated visual intermediate states <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tong et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hao et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, which introduce a costly and lossy intermediate generation process <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meng et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.
However, the role of native generation-side representations in understanding remains underexplored, particularly whether they provide complementary evidence, what enables such advantages, and how they can be used effectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first examine whether generation-side representations provide additional evidence for fine-grained understanding and what underlies any such advantage, and then develop a post-training framework to make this information effectively usable by the understanding pathway.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Systematic Analysis of Visual Representations</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare generation- and understanding-side visual representations along three dimensions: fine-grained semantic sensitivity, representational complementarity, and spatial dependence, to characterize the generation-side advantage and its underlying structure.
We study six UMMs—BAGEL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>,
Janus-Pro <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025c</a>)</cite>,
Lance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al., 2026</a>)</cite>,
Show-o2-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>,
BLIP3-o-4B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>,
and DreamLLM-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2024</a>)</cite>—and compare each model’s generation-side representation with its corresponding understanding-side visual representation.
Our analysis uses 2,084 minimally changed VisMin image pairs spanning <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">object</span>, <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">attribute</span>, <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">count</span>, and <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">relation</span>.
Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.1.2 Complementarity Analysis ‣ 3.1 Systematic Analysis of Visual Representations ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ What Generation Knows: Unlocking Fine-Grained Understanding in Unified Multimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarizes the three analyses.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.1 </span>Fine-Grained Semantic Sensitivity</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For generation-side representations to complement fine-grained understanding, they should first be sensitive to subtle semantic changes critical to these tasks.
