<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the samples in domain <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{d}=|\mathcal{D}_{d}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{d}=|\mathcal{D}_{d}|</annotation></semantics></math>. A larger <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{C}_{R}^{(d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{C}_{R}^{(d)}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates stronger semantic sensitivity after controlling for matched low-level variation.
Details of the control construction, absolute sensitivity values, and resolution analysis are provided in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Details of Representation Sensitivity Analysis ‣ What Generation Knows: Unlocking Fine-Grained Understanding in Unified Multimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.1.2 Complementarity Analysis ‣ 3.1 Systematic Analysis of Visual Representations ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ What Generation Knows: Unlocking Fine-Grained Understanding in Unified Multimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(a), generation-side representations achieve higher control-corrected sensitivity than understanding-side representations in 24 model–domain combinations, establishing an advantage in fine-grained semantic sensitivity.
On average across all six models, this advantage is smallest for <span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">count</span> and larger for <span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">attribute</span>, <span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">object</span>, and <span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">relation</span>, indicating that generation-side representations provide stronger advantages for changes that depend on local details and spatial structure than for those relying on cross-instance aggregation.
The generation-side advantage also remains positive across all tested input resolutions, confirming its robustness to input scale.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.2 </span>Complementarity Analysis</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Having established a sensitivity advantage, we next ask whether this extra responsiveness captures changes missed by understanding-side representations, since both may still respond strongly to the same visual changes.
For each sample <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, using the raw local response <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> defined above, we define the strong-response indicator as</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="q_{R}^{i}=\mathbb{I}\!\left[\rho_{R}(I_{i},I_{v,i})&gt;2\,\rho_{R}(I_{i},I_{n,i})\right],\qquad R\in\{R_{G},R_{U}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mpadded style="width:0.219em;" width="0.219em"><mi>𝕀</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{R}^{i}=\mathbb{I}\!\left[\rho_{R}(I_{i},I_{v,i})&gt;2\,\rho_{R}(I_{i},I_{n,i})\right],\qquad R\in\{R_{G},R_{U}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For each domain <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, we report the fraction where only the generation-side representation is strongly responsive as <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G\text{-only}}^{(d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-only</mtext></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G\text{-only}}^{(d)}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the fraction where only the understanding-side is strongly responsive as <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{U\text{-only}}^{(d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-only</mtext></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{U\text{-only}}^{(d)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Their difference, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\mathrm{comp}}^{(d)}=P_{G\text{-only}}^{(d)}-P_{U\text{-only}}^{(d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>comp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-only</mtext></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-only</mtext></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\mathrm{comp}}^{(d)}=P_{G\text{-only}}^{(d)}-P_{U\text{-only}}^{(d)}</annotation></semantics></math>, measures the net generation-side advantage.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.1.2 Complementarity Analysis ‣ 3.1 Systematic Analysis of Visual Representations ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ What Generation Knows: Unlocking Fine-Grained Understanding in Unified Multimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(b), <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G\text{-only}}^{(d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-only</mtext></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G\text{-only}}^{(d)}</annotation></semantics></math> exceeds <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{U\text{-only}}^{(d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-only</mtext></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{U\text{-only}}^{(d)}</annotation></semantics></math> in all 24 model–domain combinations, establishing systematic representational complementarity across all six models and four domains.
This consistent asymmetry shows that the generation-side advantage extends beyond higher overall sensitivity: generation-side representations also capture task-relevant visual changes missed by understanding-side representations.
Detailed statistics are provided in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Details of Representation Sensitivity Analysis ‣ What Generation Knows: Unlocking Fine-Grained Understanding in Unified Multimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.23987v1/figures/systematic_study.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/226;" width="548" height="226" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Control-corrected generation–understanding sensitivity gaps and strong-response complementarity across six models and four domains. (a) Solid bars show the unpermuted <math id="S3.F1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-U</annotation></semantics></math> gap, while hatched bars show the gap after spatial shuffle. (b) Stacked bars show the fractions of samples with generation-side-only and understanding-side-only strong responses.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.3 </span>Spatial Dependence</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Despite this complementarity, the conditions supporting this advantage remain unclear.
Since generation-side representations preserve rich spatial structure, we further test whether this advantage depends on spatial organization.
We apply a fixed permutation over a <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64\times 64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64\times 64</annotation></semantics></math> grid of image blocks before encoding and compare it with the unpermuted condition. We recompute control-corrected sensitivities under permutation. The detailed values are provided in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Details of Representation Sensitivity Analysis ‣ What Generation Knows: Unlocking Fine-Grained Understanding in Unified Multimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.1.2 Complementarity Analysis ‣ 3.1 Systematic Analysis of Visual Representations ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ What Generation Knows: Unlocking Fine-Grained Understanding in Unified Multimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(a), spatial permutation reduces the generation–understanding sensitivity gap in all 24 model–domain combinations, demonstrating that spatial organization is a systematic source of the generation-side advantage rather than a model- or task-specific effect.
The role of spatial organization is most direct for <span id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">relation</span>, where spatial layout defines the semantic change. Importantly, the substantial reductions for <span id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">attribute</span> and <span id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">object</span> show that this dependence is not limited to explicitly relational changes, but extends to fine-grained evidence whose usefulness depends on preserving its binding to object locations and surrounding structure.
Notably, <span id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">count</span> exhibits the smallest gap reduction on average across models, revealing a clear task-dependent pattern: the generation-side advantage is strongest when fine-grained evidence must remain locally grounded and spatially organized, and weaker when the task relies primarily on cross-instance aggregation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Generation-Assisted Understanding Framework</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our analysis reveals complementary generation-side evidence whose advantage depends on spatial organization and varies across tasks.
GAFU accordingly preserves and aligns this information in compact <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> tokens and enables task-conditioned retrieval by the understanding pathway.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1 </span>Generation Representation Compression and Alignment</h4>

<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Compression.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We adopt an encoder–decoder architecture to compress the generation-side representations into a fixed-size representation <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
The Encoder concatenates the projected generation-side representations with 256 learnable queries and feeds them into a multi-layer Transformer, where joint self-attention with spatial positional encoding allows the queries to aggregate generation information while retaining its spatial structure. The resulting query outputs are then projected to the same hidden dimension as the understanding-side tokens, forming the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> tokens.
The Decoder mirrors the Encoder: it concatenates the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> tokens with learnable reconstruction queries and feeds them into a multi-layer Transformer. The resulting query outputs are mapped through a linear prediction head to reconstruct the original generation-side representation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Alignment.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We train this architecture on Cambrian image data, freezing the native understanding and generation pathways of the UMM while optimizing only the Encoder, Decoder, and prediction heads.
The objective consists of reconstruction, spatial binding, and relation alignment losses:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ext}}=\lambda_{\mathrm{rec}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{rec}}+\lambda_{\mathrm{spa}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{spa}}+\lambda_{\mathrm{rel}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{rel}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>ext</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>rec</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>rec</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>spa</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>spa</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>rel</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>rel</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ext}}=\lambda_{\mathrm{rec}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{rec}}+\lambda_{\mathrm{spa}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{spa}}+\lambda_{\mathrm{rel}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{rel}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For reconstruction, the Decoder reconstructs the generation latent solely from <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, which is then decoded into an image <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{I}</annotation></semantics></math> through the frozen generation pathway. We define</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{rec}}=\lVert\hat{I}-I\rVert_{1}+0.5\lVert\hat{I}-I\rVert_{2}+0.5\,\mathrm{LPIPS}(\hat{I},I)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>rec</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>LPIPS</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{rec}}=\lVert\hat{I}-I\rVert_{1}+0.5\lVert\hat{I}-I\rVert_{2}+0.5\,\mathrm{LPIPS}(\hat{I},I).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">which encourages <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> to retain sufficient visual information for reconstructing the original image.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For spatial binding, we directly map each <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> token through a shared linear readout head, without passing it through the Decoder, to predict the spatially aligned generation target:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{spa}}=\tfrac{1}{N}\textstyle\sum_{i=1}^{N}\left\|h_{\mathrm{spa}}(G_{i})-z_{i}^{\mathrm{bind}}\right\|_{2}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>spa</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mstyle><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>spa</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>bind</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{spa}}=\tfrac{1}{N}\textstyle\sum_{i=1}^{N}\left\|h_{\mathrm{spa}}(G_{i})-z_{i}^{\mathrm{bind}}\right\|_{2}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\mathrm{spa}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>spa</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\mathrm{spa}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the readout head and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i}^{\mathrm{bind}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>bind</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i}^{\mathrm{bind}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the spatially aligned generation target corresponding to <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. This explicitly binds <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> tokens to the original generation representation’s spatial structure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">For relation alignment, we organize <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and the understanding-side representation <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> into two-dimensional spatial grids.
For local relation alignment at scale <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in\{4,8,16\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in\{4,8,16\}</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{(s)}=P_{s}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{(s)}=P_{s}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{(s)}=P_{s}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{(s)}=P_{s}(U)</annotation></semantics></math> by adaptive average pooling to an <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\times s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\times s</annotation></semantics></math> grid, treat each of the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> spatial locations as a node, and compare pairwise relations among these nodes within each image.
For global relation alignment, we mean-pool all spatial locations of each image into a single node and compare pairwise relations among these image-level nodes across the batch.
For a node set <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute its pairwise cosine similarity matrix, remove the diagonal entries, and center the remaining values to form the relation vector <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(Z)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The local and global relation losses are</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E7X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{local}}^{(s)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>local</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{local}}^{(s)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\tfrac{1}{B}\textstyle\sum_{b=1}^{B}\left[1-\cos\!\left(r(G_{b}^{(s)}),r(U_{b}^{(s)})\right)\right]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>B</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.216em;" width="1.216em"><mi>cos</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\tfrac{1}{B}\textstyle\sum_{b=1}^{B}\left[1-\cos\!\left(r(G_{b}^{(s)}),r(U_{b}^{(s)})\right)\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E7Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{global}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>global</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{global}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=1-\cos\!\left(r(G^{(1)}),r(U^{(1)})\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.216em;" width="1.216em"><mi>cos</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=1-\cos\!\left(r(G^{(1)}),r(U^{(1)})\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>U</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> contain one spatially mean-pooled node per image, with relations computed across the batch.
The final relation alignment loss is</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{rel}}=\tfrac{1}{4}\bigl[\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{global}}+\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{local}}^{(4)}+\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{local}}^{(8)}+\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{local}}^{(16)}\bigr]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>rel</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>global</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>local</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>local</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>local</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>16</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{rel}}=\tfrac{1}{4}\bigl[\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{global}}+\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{local}}^{(4)}+\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{local}}^{(8)}+\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{local}}^{(16)}\bigr].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.4" class="ltx_p">This aligns the relational structure of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with the understanding-side representation without directly matching feature values, preserving pathway-specific information while ensuring relational compatibility.
Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.2.2 Task-Conditioned Injection and Fine-Tuning ‣ 3.2 Generation-Assisted Understanding Framework ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ What Generation Knows: Unlocking Fine-Grained Understanding in Unified Multimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> summarizes GAFU, with training configurations provided in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Experimental Details ‣ What Generation Knows: Unlocking Fine-Grained Understanding in Unified Multimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.2 </span>Task-Conditioned Injection and Fine-Tuning</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce a task-conditioned injection framework and a CE-space pairwise DPO objective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al., 2024</a>)</cite> to encourage the model to use <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> matched with the current sample.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.23987v1/figures/framework.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/309;" width="548" height="309" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Overview of GAFU, comprising generation representation compression and alignment (Stage 1) and task-conditioned injection (Stage 2).</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Task-Conditioned Injection.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After each Transformer layer in the understanding pathway, we use current text hidden states as queries and projected <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> tokens as keys and values in cross-attention, with <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> using the same positional encoding as native understanding-side visual tokens.
The cross-attention output is residually added to the text hidden states and passed to the next layer, allowing selective retrieval of task-relevant information from <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> as the textual context evolves.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training Objective.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each training sample <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, we construct a positive branch using its matched <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and a shuffle-<math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> branch that replaces <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\pi(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\pi(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> from another image.
We optimize</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\lambda_{\mathrm{CE}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CE}}+\lambda_{\mathrm{DPO}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{DPO}}+\lambda_{\mathrm{KL}}(t)\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{KL}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>CE</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>CE</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>DPO</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>DPO</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\lambda_{\mathrm{CE}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CE}}+\lambda_{\mathrm{DPO}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{DPO}}+\lambda_{\mathrm{KL}}(t)\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{KL}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>CE</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CE}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the standard answer cross-entropy loss on the positive branch, providing the basic supervision for task learning.
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{DPO}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>DPO</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{DPO}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a pairwise preference objective defined in CE space, encouraging the positive branch to achieve lower answer CE than the shuffle-<math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> branch and thereby promoting the use of sample-specific information from <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{KL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{KL}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a forward KL loss between the shuffle-<math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> branch, which receives the correct <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> but a mismatched <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, and the frozen original model conditioned on <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> alone, preserving the original output distribution under mismatched <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and preventing DPO from enlarging the preference gap by degrading the shuffle-<math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> branch.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond these objectives, we adopt several strategies to stabilize preference learning and encourage use of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
To stabilize DPO training, we weight positive and negative DPO gradients by <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4:1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4:1</annotation></semantics></math> and cap the negative gradient norm at <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.25</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.25</annotation></semantics></math> times that of the matched-<math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> positive branch, which combines answer-CE and positive DPO gradients, preventing margin growth by degrading the shuffled branch.
The weight of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{KL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{KL}}</annotation></semantics></math> is gradually decayed to zero, stabilizing early preference learning while avoiding over-constraining the model to the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>-only reference later in training.
In early training, we mask <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> for a subset of samples to reduce reliance on the original understanding pathway.
Detailed loss definitions, gradient decomposition, and training schedules are provided in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Task-Conditioned Injection and Fine-Tuning ‣ What Generation Knows: Unlocking Fine-Grained Understanding in Unified Multimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
