<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>General Fine-grained Visual Understanding</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate whether GAFU provides consistent gains beyond SFT for general fine-grained visual understanding across UMM architectures.
As shown in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Training Objective. ‣ 3.2.2 Task-Conditioned Injection and Fine-Tuning ‣ 3.2 Generation-Assisted Understanding Framework ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ What Generation Knows: Unlocking Fine-Grained Understanding in Unified Multimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, GAFU outperforms SFT on all three benchmarks across BAGEL, Janus-Pro, and Lance, improving VStar-Bench by 5.23–7.85 percentage points, OmniSpatial by 2.61–4.44 points, and MMVP by 3.67–5.67 points.
The consistent gains from 3B Lance to 14B BAGEL show that generation-side complementarity benefits fine-grained visual understanding across model scales, UMM architectures, and representation pathways.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Across all nine model–benchmark combinations, the improvement of our method over SFT is larger than that of SFT over the Base model. This contrast is particularly evident on MMVP, where SFT brings no improvement to Janus-Pro and slightly degrades Lance, whereas our method achieves further gains, suggesting that supervised adaptation relying solely on the native understanding pathway cannot reliably overcome its fine-grained visual representation bottleneck, while complementary generation-side information provides additional effective visual evidence.
The largest gains on VStar-Bench indicate that generation-side representations provide complementary evidence for local attributes, small objects, and relative positions that can be weakened by visual compression in the understanding pathway. Consistent OmniSpatial gains show that this complementarity extends to spatial layouts and inter-object relations, enabling finer spatial reasoning and showing that this advantage spans local details and spatial relations rather than one type of visual evidence.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Domain-Specific Visual Understanding</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We further examine whether generation-side information remains beneficial after domain-specific adaptation.
As shown in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Training Objective. ‣ 3.2.2 Task-Conditioned Injection and Fine-Tuning ‣ 3.2 Generation-Assisted Understanding Framework ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ What Generation Knows: Unlocking Fine-Grained Understanding in Unified Multimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, our method outperforms SFT on both tasks across all three models, improving PathVQA by 1.12–7.65 percentage points and VSR-Bench by 1.09–6.15 points.
These consistent post-adaptation gains show that domain supervision does not fully capture the complementary visual evidence available in generation-side representations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The two domains show distinct gain patterns, reflecting different roles of generation-side information.
On PathVQA, SFT already provides substantial gains over the Base models, yet our method further improves upon these strong SFT baselines, indicating that although domain supervision enables substantial medical-domain and task adaptation, it does not fully resolve the use of fine-grained visual evidence, which can be further complemented by generation-side information.
In contrast, VSR-Bench shows a sharper pattern: SFT yields only marginal gains over Base, whereas GAFU provides clear additional gains, indicating that the remaining limitation is more strongly tied to spatial and relational representation than to task adaptation. The structural and spatial information retained by generation-side representations directly addresses this limitation.
Representative qualitative examples across general and domain-specific tasks are shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.3 Domain-Specific Visual Understanding ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ What Generation Knows: Unlocking Fine-Grained Understanding in Unified Multimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.23987v1/figures/Casestudy.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/292;" width="548" height="292" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>
<span id="S4.F3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GAFU enhances fine-grained visual understanding.</span>
Examples show better recognition of fine-grained details, spatial relations,
and domain-specific cues over the base model and SFT.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Ablation Study</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct all ablations on BAGEL under the general setting, covering alignment objectives, injection interfaces, injection density, and fine-tuning loss.
For alignment objectives, we remove spatial binding and relation alignment separately or together, with reconstruction unchanged.
For injection interfaces, we compare concatenating <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with the native understanding-side visual tokens <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, querying <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, current visual hidden states, or input-independent learnable queries. Our method queries <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with current text hidden states.
For injection density, we compare all 27 Transformer layers with 10 evenly spaced layers.
For fine-tuning loss, we compare CE only, CE + DPO, and the full CE + DPO + KL objective.
Results are reported in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Evaluation. ‣ 4.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ What Generation Knows: Unlocking Fine-Grained Understanding in Unified Multimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Alignment Objective.</span>
OmniSpatial highlights the role of spatial binding in preserving spatial correspondences, dropping by 1.37 points without it, while VStar-Bench shows that fine-grained evidence requires relation alignment with the understanding pathway, dropping by 1.57 points without it.
MMVP further reveals their complementarity: combining both constraints outperforms either by 4.67–5.34 points, showing that their joint use is key to exploiting generation-side information.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Injection Interface.</span>
How <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is read matters more than merely making it available to the model.
Concatenating <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, or using <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> to query <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> at input, trails ours by 1.96–6.28 and 4.67–12.14 points, respectively, suggesting that premature fusion can interfere with understanding representations before the language-side task state is formed.
Visual hidden-state queries outperform input-level fusion but remain 1.05–4.67 points below text hidden-state queries across benchmarks. Learnable queries nearly match ours on OmniSpatial with a 0.33-point gap but lag by 4.71 and 4.00 points on VStar-Bench and MMVP, showing that task-independent queries can capture stable spatial structure, whereas selecting question-specific evidence requires task context from text hidden states.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Injection Density.</span>
10-layer sparse injection remains within 0.00–1.67 points of full-layer performance, showing that injection frequency matters less than how <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is organized and accessed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fine-Tuning Loss.</span>
DPO improves VStar-Bench and MMVP by 1.05 and 8.00 points but degrades OmniSpatial by 11.09 points without KL. The full objective then improves all three benchmarks by 3.14–12.27 points over CE + DPO, showing that DPO promotes sample-specific <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> use while KL is essential for turning preference learning into reliable gains.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present GAFU, a generation-assisted understanding post-training framework for fine-grained visual understanding in UMMs.
First, we systematically examine the role of native generation-side
representations in fine-grained understanding and the conditions that enable it.
Second, based on these findings, GAFU preserves and organizes complementary generation-side information through compression, spatial binding, and relation alignment, selectively retrieves it through task-conditioned injection, and promotes the use of sample-relevant evidence through pairwise preference learning.
Third, across UMM architectures, GAFU consistently outperforms same-data SFT on general, spatial, and domain-specific visual understanding tasks, with maximum relative gains of 20.00%, 17.94%, and 8.62% on VStar-Bench, OmniSpatial, and VSR-Bench, respectively.
Overall, GAFU converts complementary information in native generation-side representations into usable visual evidence for the understanding pathway, yielding consistent gains in fine-grained visual understanding.
We hope this work will help UMMs move beyond joint modeling of understanding and generation toward deeper capability synergy, and provide a new path toward more reliable and fine-grained visual understanding in complex real-world scenarios.</p>
</div>
</section>
