<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Details of Representation Sensitivity Analysis</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Representation Extraction and Preprocessing</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We extract the understanding-side representation <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> and generation-side representation <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> from each UMM as follows:</p>
<ul id="A1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">BAGEL.</span> <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> comprises the SigLIP visual tokens after the connector (<math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16\times 16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16\times 16</annotation></semantics></math>). <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained from the KL-VAE posterior mean (<math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64\times 64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64\times 64</annotation></semantics></math>) through <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 2</annotation></semantics></math> patchification, yielding a <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32\times 32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32\times 32</annotation></semantics></math> grid.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Janus-Pro.</span> <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> comprises the SigLIP-L/16 visual tokens after the aligner, and <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> comprises the quantized VQ-16 codebook embeddings <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\mathrm{vq}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>vq</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\mathrm{vq}}</annotation></semantics></math>, both on <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="24\times 24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>24</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">24\times 24</annotation></semantics></math> grids.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lance.</span> <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> comprises the merged Qwen visual tokens (<math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16\times 16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16\times 16</annotation></semantics></math>), and <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is the posterior mean of the frozen Wan2.2-VAE (<math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32\times 32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32\times 32</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Show-o2-7B.</span> <math id="A1.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is extracted from the output of
<span id="A1.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">image_embedder_und</span> after adding the native visual positional
embedding and applying <span id="A1.I1.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">und_trans</span>. <math id="A1.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is extracted from the
output of <span id="A1.I1.i4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">image_embedder_gen</span>. Both representations are
adaptively average-pooled to a common <math id="A1.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16\times 16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16\times 16</annotation></semantics></math> grid. The resulting
channel dimensions are 1152 for <math id="A1.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> and 3584 for <math id="A1.I1.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">BLIP3-o-4B.</span> <math id="A1.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> comprises the merged Qwen2.5-VL visual tokens (<math id="A1.I1.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16\times 16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16\times 16</annotation></semantics></math>), and <math id="A1.I1.i5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> comprises the penultimate-layer patch features of the EVA-CLIP-E/14-plus generation vision tower (<math id="A1.I1.i5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32\times 32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32\times 32</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DreamLLM-7B.</span> <math id="A1.I1.i6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> comprises the penultimate-layer CLIP ViT-L/14 patch features after the native projector, excluding the CLS token (<math id="A1.I1.i6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16\times 16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16\times 16</annotation></semantics></math>). <math id="A1.I1.i6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is the generation VAE posterior mean with the native latent scaling factor applied (<math id="A1.I1.i6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64\times 64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64\times 64</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We preserve the spatial arrangement of each representation and align its grid to <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16\times 16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16\times 16</annotation></semantics></math> using adaptive average pooling, yielding the position-wise features <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{R}(I,p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{R}(I,p)</annotation></semantics></math> used in the main text. For VAE-based representations, each non-overlapping <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 2</annotation></semantics></math> latent
block is concatenated along the channel dimension to form one patch feature before adaptive pooling. Representations that are already provided as patch-level features, such as Show-o2-7B and BLIP3-o-4B, are pooled directly from their native feature grids. This procedure standardizes the spatial grid without requiring <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> to have the same feature dimension.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the sensitivity metric, cosine similarity uses <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\mathrm{cos}}=10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>cos</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\mathrm{cos}}=10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math>. Local responses are averaged within contiguous <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 2</annotation></semantics></math> windows with stride one and no padding, followed by maximum aggregation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Control-Corrected Construction</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our goal is to construct a control image <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> for each semantic edit <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\rightarrow I_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\rightarrow I_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> that has the same visual change intensity within the same edited region, while preserving natural appearance and subject semantics.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We first identify the edited region from the pixel differences between <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each pixel <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute the mean squared RGB difference:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(p)=\frac{1}{3}\sum_{c=1}^{3}\bigl(I_{v}(p,c)-I(p,c)\bigr)^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></munderover><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(p)=\frac{1}{3}\sum_{c=1}^{3}\bigl(I_{v}(p,c)-I(p,c)\bigr)^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We select the 25% of nonzero pixels with the largest differences and apply two binary dilations to obtain the edited-region mask <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. All control changes are confined to <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, while pixels outside <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> remain identical to the original image.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We convert the images to linear RGB and denote the original and semantic-edit images by <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>. The RMS within the edited region is defined as:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{RMS}_{M}(A)=\sqrt{\frac{1}{3|M|}\sum_{p\in M}\sum_{c=1}^{3}A(p,c)^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>RMS</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></munderover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{RMS}_{M}(A)=\sqrt{\frac{1}{3|M|}\sum_{p\in M}\sum_{c=1}^{3}A(p,c)^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We generate candidates using predefined single structural-preserving natural degradation operators, including white- or black-endpoint mixing, gamma curves, local-mean smoothing, regional-mean replacement, horizontal or vertical illumination redistribution, spatial illumination fields, piecewise tone curves, luminance lifting, and local-contrast adjustment. These operators do not translate, scale, deform, add, remove, or redraw the subject.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">For a valid endpoint <math id="A1.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> of an operator, we define its local direction as:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=w\odot(J-X),\qquad w(p)=M(p)\min\left(\frac{\operatorname{dist}(p,\partial M)}{8},1\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>8</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=w\odot(J-X),\qquad w(p)=M(p)\min\left(\frac{\operatorname{dist}(p,\partial M)}{8},1\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">The control image is then solved in closed form along this single direction:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{n}=X+\frac{\operatorname{RMS}_{M}(X_{v}-X)}{\operatorname{RMS}_{M}(D)}D." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>RMS</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>RMS</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{n}=X+\frac{\operatorname{RMS}_{M}(X_{v}-X)}{\operatorname{RMS}_{M}(D)}D.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, <math id="A1.SS2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> have the same linear-RGB RMS within the edited region. We require <math id="A1.SS2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{n}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{n}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and discard candidates that violate this constraint. Each operator produces one candidate; the remaining candidate closest to the original image under LPIPS is selected as the final control image.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Complementarity Analysis</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Using the strong-response criterion defined in the main text,
Table <a href="#A1.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ A.3 Complementarity Analysis ‣ Appendix A Details of Representation Sensitivity Analysis ‣ What Generation Knows: Unlocking Fine-Grained Understanding in Unified Multimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> reports the proportions of samples with
generation-side-only and understanding-side-only strong responses, together
with their difference.
Across all 24 model–domain combinations, the proportion of generation-side-only
strong responses is higher than that of understanding-side-only strong responses.
The average values of
<math id="A1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G\text{-only}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-only</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G\text{-only}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{U\text{-only}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-only</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{U\text{-only}}</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="28.18\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>28.18</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">28.18\%</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.48\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.48</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.48\%</annotation></semantics></math>,
respectively, and the average net difference is <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25.70" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>25.70</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25.70</annotation></semantics></math> percentage points.
This result indicates that generation-side representations exhibit unique
strong responses to a broad range of fine-grained visual changes.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Across domains, attribute and object have the highest
<math id="A1.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G\text{-only}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-only</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G\text{-only}}</annotation></semantics></math> values, at <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="35.66\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>35.66</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">35.66\%</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="33.19\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>33.19</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">33.19\%</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
This indicates that generation-side-only responses occur more frequently for
attribute- and object-related changes. The generation-side advantages in the
count and relation domains are relatively weaker, with values of <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="23.29\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>23.29</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">23.29\%</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20.58\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>20.58</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20.58\%</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, consistent with the smaller <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-U</annotation></semantics></math> gaps in
these two domains observed in the sensitivity experiments.</p>
</div>
