<section id="A1.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.5 </span>Spatial Dependence</h3>

<div id="A1.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To test whether the generation-side response depends on the original spatial organization, we apply a fixed block-level spatial permutation before encoding.
Because the six models use different input canvases, permutation is
performed on a <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="512\times 512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>512</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">512\times 512</annotation></semantics></math> canvas for BAGEL, Lance, and DreamLLM-7B,
on a <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="384\times 384" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>384</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>384</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">384\times 384</annotation></semantics></math> canvas for Janus-Pro and Show-o2-7B, and on a
<math id="A1.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="448\times 448" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>448</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>448</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">448\times 448</annotation></semantics></math> canvas for BLIP3-o-4B. In all cases, the canvas is
partitioned into a fixed <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64\times 64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64\times 64</annotation></semantics></math> grid of image blocks, corresponding
to <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8\times 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8\times 8</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6\times 6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6\times 6</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7\times 7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7\times 7</annotation></semantics></math>-pixel blocks, respectively.
The permuted and unpermuted conditions use identical representation
extraction, common-grid alignment, and control-correction procedures.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A1.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ A.5 Spatial Dependence ‣ Appendix A Details of Representation Sensitivity Analysis ‣ What Generation Knows: Unlocking Fine-Grained Understanding in Unified Multimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> reports the control-corrected responses before and after spatial permutation.</p>
</div>
