</section>
<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2 </span>Training</h3>

<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Stage 1: Compression and alignment.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the AdamW optimizer with a peak learning rate of <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>, warmed up linearly over the first 200 optimizer steps. The learning rate then follows a cosine decay schedule to a minimum of <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>. We set the weight decay to 0.01, <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=(0.9,0.95)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.95</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=(0.9,0.95)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=10^{-8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=10^{-8}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the maximum gradient norm to 1.0. Training uses 8 GPUs, with a micro-batch size of 4 per GPU, an effective global batch size of 32, and 8,000 optimizer steps.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Stage 2: Task-conditioned injection and fine-tuning.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Training uses an effective global batch size of 32 for 5,000 optimizer steps. The injection projector and internal attention modules use a peak learning rate of <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>, while the understanding-pathway LoRA uses <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>. Both learning rates are linearly warmed up over the first 500 optimizer steps and then follow cosine decay to <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. The LoRA rank and alpha are both set to 16. We use AdamW with a weight decay of 0.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To verify whether the model uses sample-specific <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> tokens, we periodically evaluate the real branch and the shuffle-<math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> branch on a fixed validation subset. The real branch uses the matched generation memory <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas the shuffle-<math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> branch replaces it with <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\pi(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\pi(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> while keeping the question, answer, and native understanding representation unchanged. Figure <a href="#A4.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Stage 2: Task-conditioned injection and fine-tuning. ‣ D.2 Training ‣ Appendix D Experimental Details ‣ What Generation Knows: Unlocking Fine-Grained Understanding in Unified Multimodal Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> reports the answer-token CE of the real and shuffle-<math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> branches and their CE gap under three fine-tuning objectives: CE only, CE + DPO, and CE + DPO + KL. The CE gap is defined as
<math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{shuffle}-G}-\mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{real}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>CE</mi><mrow><mi>shuffle</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>CE</mi><mi>real</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{shuffle}-G}-\mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{real}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The CE-only configuration maintains only a small positive CE gap, indicating that the model makes limited use of sample-specific <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> tokens. Introducing DPO explicitly optimizes the CE difference between matched and mismatched generation memory, encouraging the model to assign lower answer-token CE to the matched <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and thereby increasing the CE gap. However, this objective may enlarge the gap by excessively degrading the shuffle-<math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> branch. The shuffle-<math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> KL regularization addresses this issue by constraining the output distribution of the shuffled branch to remain close to that of the frozen reference model, protecting the shuffled branch from uncontrolled deviation while preserving the positive CE gap. Thus, DPO promotes the use of sample-specific generation information, whereas KL regularization controls the cost of using mismatched generation memory.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.23987v1/figures/stage2_validation.png" id="A4.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/239;" width="548" height="239" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>Stage 2 validation dynamics under different fine-tuning objectives.
The three columns correspond to CE only, CE + DPO, and CE + DPO + KL,
respectively. The first row reports the answer-token CE of the real and
shuffle-<math id="A4.F8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> branches, while the second row reports their CE gap,
defined as <math id="A4.F8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{shuffle}-G}-\mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{real}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>CE</mi><mrow><mi>shuffle</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>CE</mi><mi>real</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{shuffle}-G}-\mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{real}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The real branch uses matched <math id="A4.F8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>
tokens, whereas the shuffle-<math id="A4.F8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> branch uses mismatched <math id="A4.F8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> tokens
from another sample. A positive CE gap indicates that the model assigns
lower answer-token cross-entropy to matched <math id="A4.F8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> tokens.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.3 </span>Evaluation</h3>

<div id="A4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The evaluation protocols for the benchmarks are as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="A4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">VStar-Bench</span>: We use 191 multiple-choice samples and shuffle the option positions in the formal manifest. For each candidate answer, we compute the average answer-token CE and select the candidate with the lowest CE. Option labels are normalized to uppercase A–D, and we report choice accuracy.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">OmniSpatial</span>: We use 1,533 spatial-reasoning samples. The main protocol uses forced-choice scoring based on the average candidate-token CE. For each candidate answer, we compute the average token CE and select the candidate with the lowest CE.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MMVP</span>: We use 300 questions forming 150 complementary question pairs. For each candidate answer, we compute the average token CE and select the candidate with the lowest CE.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">VSR-Bench</span>: We use 2,195 samples from the random test split and apply the True/False greedy-generation protocol.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PathVQA</span>: We use all 6,719 samples from the official test set for medical visual question answering. We use greedy decoding without sampling until EOS is generated or the maximum number of new tokens is reached. We report normalized exact-match accuracy, where generated and reference answers are lowercased and stripped of punctuation before exact matching.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Limitations</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">First, GAFU currently targets UMMs with mainstream dual-pathway visual encoders and
does not cover all UMM architectures.
Its application to models with different visual pathway designs or incompatible
representation interfaces requires additional adaptation.
Second, the effectiveness of GAFU depends on the quality of the
native generation representations, and the resulting benefit may vary across models and tasks.
Third, despite its performance gains, extracting, projecting, and injecting the
generation tokens introduces extra inference computation and memory overhead
compared with using the native understanding pathway alone.</p>
</div>
</section>
